
pjpi — Geophysical / Petrophysical Inversion Utilities
=====================================================

Short description
-----------------
`pjpi` is a Python library for coupled geophysical and petrophysical
inversions using Gauss‑Newton based workflows. It provides utilities for
mesh and model handling, regularisation operators, and interfaces to
`pygimli` forward operators (ERT, traveltime, etc.), plus example scripts
and helper functions for demonstrations and research.

Features
--------
- Managers for geophysical and petrophysical inversion workflows
- Utilities for mesh and model processing (`gnmesh.meshtools`)
- Multiple regularisers (damping, spatial smoothing, X-gradient)
- Example/demo scripts under `example_gn/` including `run_all_inversions.py`
- Integration with `pygimli` forward operators

Quickstart — Conda-based installation
-------------------------------------
Recommended: create and use a Conda environment (helps with `pygimli` and
other compiled dependencies).

Create environment and install dependencies:

```bash
# create environment (and install pygimli)
conda create -n pjpi
conda activate pjpi

conda install -c gimli pygimli

# optinally install cupy into this environment for cupy support
conda install -c conda-forge cupy

# Install package in editable/development mode (from project root)
cd pjpi
pip install -e .
```

If you prefer to use `mamba` for faster installs, replace `conda install`
with `mamba install`.

Example: run all example inversions
----------------------------------
Run the example inversion scripts sequentially (non-parallel):

```bash
# from project root
python example_gn/run_full_example.py

# or run and stop on first error
./example_gn/scripts_inversion/run_all_inversions.py --stop
```

Contribution
------------
Contributions are welcome. For larger changes, open an issue first and
create Pull Requests with clear descriptions and tests. Follow the project
style: library code should use `logging` rather than `print` for messages.

Citation & contact
------------------
See `CITATION.cff` for citation instructions. For questions or major
changes, open an issue or contact the maintainers via GitHub.

